Abstract Factory — Quick Theory

Purpose:
The Abstract Factory Pattern is used to create objects without directly coupling the client
to the concrete classes.

In your example:

Employee
   ↑
   ├── AndroidDeveloper
   └── WebDeveloper

EmployeeAbstractFactory
   ↑
   ├── AndroidDevFactory
   └── WebDevFactory

EmployeeFactory
   └── getEmployee(factory)

What each class does
Class	                        Responsibility
Employee	            Common interface for all employees
AndroidDeveloper	    Concrete Android employee
WebDeveloper	        Concrete Web employee
EmployeeAbstractFactory	Defines createEmployee()
AndroidDevFactory	    Creates AndroidDeveloper
WebDevFactory	        Creates WebDeveloper
EmployeeFactory	        Gives the employee object to the client

Most important idea
Instead of doing this directly: Employee e = new AndroidDeveloper();
you do: Employee e = EmployeeFactory.getEmployee(new AndroidDevFactory());
So the client doesn't directly create AndroidDeveloper.

The creation responsibility is moved to the factory:

                AndroidDevFactory
                        ↓
                new AndroidDeveloper()

One-line memory trick 🧠
Interface → Concrete Classes → Concrete Factories → Factory → Client

Or even shorter: "Factory decides which object to create; client only asks for the object."

Why your code is called Abstract Factory?
EmployeeAbstractFactory defines the common creation method:

abstract class EmployeeAbstractFactory {
    public abstract Employee createEmployee();
}

Then each concrete factory decides which implementation to create:
AndroidDevFactory → AndroidDeveloper
WebDevFactory     → WebDeveloper
Quick interview definition

Abstract Factory is a creational design pattern that provides an interface for creating related
objects without specifying their concrete classes.